pose $X$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S.$\n\nThe values $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ are called the **first moment** and **second moment** of $X,$ respectively. \n\nRecall that the first moment $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is also called the *expected value* of $X$ and is computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo find second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ the expected value of the the random variable $X^2,$ we simply substitute $x^2$ for $x$ in the formula above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's compute the second moment $\\textrm{E}[X^2]$ of the continuous random variable $X$ with the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, \\qquad 0 \\leq x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of $X^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{7/2}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{2}{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1065 6591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2847fd82-e842-5ae8-889a-66f29c0442ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158562 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1064 "It's also possible to define higher moments. For example, $\\textrm{E}[X^3]$ is the **third moment** of the random variable $X.$\n\nNotice the pattern:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X] = \\int_S x \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *first* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^2] = \\int_S x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *second* moment\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\textrm{E}[X^3] = \\int_S x^3 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x$ is the *third* moment\n\nand so on. In general, the **$n$th moment** is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X^n] = \\int_S x^n \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, note that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^2], \\quad \\textrm{E}[X^3], \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nare sometimes referred to as the first, second, and third **raw moments**. This is because it's possible to define other types of moments.\n\nIn general, $\\textrm{E}[X^n]$ can be called either the $n$th *moment* or $n$th *raw moment*." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1065 6592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e3527d5e-b0d3-5d76-aa5f-5bc7ffcfd66b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158563 #edb/ref 1067 "Higher Moments" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427291 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427291 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427291 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "33cb4776-7d73-5e89-be66-3abb80c22cd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427301 #edb/ref 1080 "1-\\frac{1}{e}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427301 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427301 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d5bf664-a18f-566d-a4f7-3bd738b55f2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427309 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}+2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427309 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427309 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74d74ac0-e401-549d-95e1-5b551db31568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427322 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{e}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427322 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427322 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aa2240d8-61b4-5dec-86e4-1ecedc83ab1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427333 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{e}-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ce1156f5-fddd-5d6b-adf1-9732ee27312f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186427333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427348 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427348 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427348 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186427291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427348 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "62e8ebaf-8a75-5cb6-8621-5bcf5f7ac9e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427364 #edb/ref 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,{e}^{-2}<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}},$ compute $\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "63bcffd8-dbf0-5c48-8ca6-56531c05d1c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427364 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X]=4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{e}^{-2}}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{e}^{-2}}^{1}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,|}_{{e}^{-2}}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[4{X}^{2}-2X] & =4\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}})-2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}-1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
